Here are the things that are wrong with the snippet:

 1. SQL Injection  
    • You are concatenating `request.params.id` directly into your SQL.  An attacker can call  
      `/users/1; DROP TABLE users;–` (or similar) and execute arbitrary SQL.  
    • Always use parameterized queries / prepared statements instead of string‐concatenation.

 2. No Input Validation / Type Checking  
    • You never check that `id` is really an integer (or whatever type your `users.id` column uses).  
    • You should at minimum coerce and verify:  
       – In JavaScript:  
         ```js
         const userId = parseInt(request.params.id, 10);
         if (Number.isNaN(userId) || userId < 1) {
           return response.status(400).send({ error: "invalid id" });
         }
         ```  

 3. SELECT *  
    • Returning `*` will give you every column (password hash, auth tokens, maybe PII).  
    • Always list only the columns you actually need, e.g.:  
      ```sql
      SELECT id, username, email
      FROM users
      WHERE id = ?
      LIMIT 1
      ```  

 4. No Authorization Check  
    • Even if `id` is well-formed, you need to make sure that the caller is allowed to see that user.  
    • In most systems a user can only fetch their own record (or an admin can fetch anyone’s).  
    • Add middleware or logic to verify `request.user.id === userId` (or roles check).

 5. No Error Handling  
    • What if the query fails?  What if there is no matching user?  
    • You should catch database errors, return 404 when no user is found, and log unexpected errors.  

Putting it all together, in Node/Express with a MySQL or PostgreSQL client it might look like:

```js
app.get("/users/:id", async (req, res) => {
  // 1. Validate & parse
  const userId = parseInt(req.params.id, 10);
  if (!Number.isInteger(userId) || userId < 1) {
    return res.status(400).json({ error: "Invalid user ID" });
  }

  // 2. Authorization (example: only self or admin)
  if (req.user.id !== userId && !req.user.isAdmin) {
    return res.status(403).json({ error: "Forbidden" });
  }

  try {
    // 3. Parameterized query (no SQL injection)
    const result = await db.query(
      "SELECT id, username, email FROM users WHERE id = $1 LIMIT 1",
      [userId]
    );

    if (result.rows.length === 0) {
      return res.status(404).json({ error: "User not found" });
    }

    // 4. Return only needed data
    return res.json(result.rows[0]);

  } catch (err) {
    console.error("Database error fetching user:", err);
    return res.status(500).json({ error: "Internal server error" });
  }
});
```

Summary of fixes:  
– Never concatenate user input into SQL. Use prepared statements or an ORM.  
– Validate and cast `id`.  
– Return only specific fields.  
– Enforce authorization rules.  
– Handle “not found” vs. database errors cleanly.
